{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/tbl-knot-centric-table-table2-data","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Table","doco:Table"],"label":"tbl:knot-centric-table-table2-data","kind":"table","title":"Data table 2 from rem:knot-centric-table","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-knot-centric-table"],"tags":["extracted-table"],"text":"knot-centric-table-table2-data.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"tbl:knot-centric-table-table2-data\"></a>\n<strong>Table.</strong> <em>Data table 2 from rem:knot-centric-table</em></p>\n<p><strong>Proton sub-shell filling sequence per row.</strong> This table gives the\ncolumn ordering used by the knot-centric periodic table\n(<a href=\"#rem:knot-centric-table\">knot-centric table</a>): for each nuclear\nrow, the Mayer--Jensen sub-shells are listed in their left-to-right\nfilling order, with each sub-level <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">nl_j</code> carrying its proton\ncapacity <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2j + 1</code> in parentheses and the row total giving the shell\ncapacity that closes at the next magic number.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"right\">Row</th>\n<th>Sub-shells (capacity)</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">1</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1s_{1/2}</code>(2)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">2</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1p_{3/2}</code>(4) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1p_{1/2}</code>(2) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1d_{5/2}</code>(2)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">3</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1d_{5/2}</code>(4) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2s_{1/2}</code>(2) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1d_{3/2}</code>(4)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">4</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1f_{7/2}</code>(8)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">5</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2p_{3/2}</code>(4) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1f_{5/2}</code>(6) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2p_{1/2}</code>(2) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1g_{9/2}</code>(10)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">6</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1g_{7/2}</code>(8) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2d_{5/2}</code>(6) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2d_{3/2}</code>(4) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3s_{1/2}</code>(2) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1h_{11/2}</code>(12)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\">7</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2f_{7/2}</code>(8) <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mid</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1h_{9/2}</code>(2)</td>\n<td align=\"right\">10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Reading each row left to right recovers the column structure of the\ntable: the far-left sub-shell is the magic-receptive entry — the first\nproton past the previous stability pair — and the far-right sub-shell\ncloses the row at its magic number.</p>\n"}
